<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ruhnama</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2001">2001</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a> · Also known as Book of the Soul</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ruhnama, or 'Book of the Soul', is a book of spiritual and moral guidance, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> history and personal reflection published under the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a>'s president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saparmurat-niyazov">Saparmurat Niyazov</a>, with a first volume in 2001 and a second in 2004. It became a pillar of his personality cult: it was compulsory reading in schools, universities and workplaces and was even required for driving tests. Its role was reduced after his death in 2006.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Ruhnama was written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saparmurat-niyazov">Saparmurat Niyazov</a>, president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a> from 1990 to 2006. Epics had long played important roles in the social life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> had considered them politically suspicious: almost all significant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> epics were condemned and banned under Joseph Stalin, and were rehabilitated only with glasnost. Niyazov claimed to have received a prophetic vision in which eminent Turkmen ancestors urged him to lead the Turkmen to the golden path of life. A first version released in the 1990s was soon withdrawn. The first volume was published in December 2001, and a second followed in September 2004. Victoria Clement and Riccardo Nicolosi suspect that the work was ghost-written.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Scholars describe the book as a mosaic of genres: spiritual and political advice, legends, autobiography, short stories, poems and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> history. The first volume borrows stories and proverbs from the Oğuzname, the Book of Dede Korkut and the Epic of Koroghlu to preach virtues such as generosity, unity and hospitality. It presents the Turkmen as descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noah">Noah</a> through Oghuz Khagan and credits them with some seventy states, among them the Parthian, Achaemenid and Seljuk empires. It cites no source material in an academic sense. Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a> is described in a single page, and Niyazov's own life is told in detail.</p><p>The second volume was drafted with explicit parallels to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> and asked that the Ruhnama be recited as a regular prayer after purification. Its 21 chapters deal with manners and conduct for different situations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The book was placed first in schools and libraries and then made mandatory reading in schools, universities and government organisations. New government employees were tested on it at interviews, and an examination on it became part of the driving test. In 2002 the months and days of the week were renamed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> after parts of the Ruhnama, September taking the name of the book itself. Mosques and churches were made to display it as prominently as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>. In August 2005 the first volume was launched into orbit. In 2004 schools were assigned two to four hours a week to it, and 26 of the 57 examination cards for the 2006 university entrance examination drew on it.</p><p>After Niyazov's death in December 2006 official references to the Ruhnama were trimmed, and from about 2009 television broadcasts of it stopped. The requirement to pass a secondary-school examination on it was rescinded in 2011, and in 2014 universities stopped testing applicants on it. It has been translated into over 50 languages.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ruhnama” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Niyazov styled himself Turkmenbashi, 'leader of the Turkmen', and created an elaborate personality cult, with golden statues and his book <b>Ruhnama</b> required reading.</blockquote><a href="/state/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a></li></ul></section>
